KZ3 patch 1126 (SPU pass 106): upstream ps3recomp PR #489 by erikomis (MIT, head b3a75d98), "each host thread runs
HLE-to-guest callbacks on a stack of its own", ported onto KZ3's ppu_loader patches (1009, 1020, 1080, 1116, ...).

ppu_guest_call / ppu_guest_call_ct started every callback at the fixed EA 0xCFFE0000 on every host thread (the
PPU_THREAD_LOCAL held one constant), so callbacks delivered at the same time from two host threads (KZ3: GCM
vblank/flip from the ticker, others from the main and decoder threads) and a callback nested in another wrote over
each other's frames; 0xCFFE0000 also lies below the stack region, in the RSX local window cellGcmSys reports
(0xC0000000). Now the top 16 MB of the stack region (VM_CB_STACK_BASE = 0xDF000000) is 64 slots of 256 KB, one per
host thread on its first callback (more than 64: the last slot is shared, logged once per thread as
"[ppu] guest_call: more than 64 host threads ... SHARED"); a nested callback starts 0x400 below the outer one's sp.
vm_stack_allocate stops at VM_CB_STACK_BASE (240 MB left for guest thread stacks: KZ3's 53 threads take ~1.7 MB in a
K4 run, spu-r105c-a1's sys_ppu_thread_create stack= sizes; bump allocator). KZ3's main stack is at 0x0FF00000 (port
main.cpp STACK_TOP), outside the region. ASSUMPTION: no guest fiber switch happens inside a callback on one host
thread (a second fiber's callback would start at the slot top again, as all callbacks did before).
Also: patch 1020's g_pt_val read in vm_read32 moves inside its #ifdef _WIN32 (the variable is Windows-only), so the
POSIX host tests that build the real ppu_loader.cpp compile; no change on Windows.
Test: the PR's runtime/ppu/tests/test_ppu_guest_call_stack.cpp + tools/test_ppu_guest_call_stack.py (5 checks), via
tools/spu/test_r106_guest_call_stack.sh.

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.18.0 (SPU pass 108): shrinks to 1020's `g_pt_val` read under `_WIN32` (callback stacks = upstream #489).

